    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Brown City began as a railroad station in 1879 on the Port Huron and Northwestern Railway, later part of the Pere Marquette system.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown_City%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "By 1907, Brown City had officially become a city after incorporating as a village in 1887.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://thumbwind.com/2026/03/10/brief-history-of-brown-city/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town earned the nickname “Home of the Motorhome” after the Travco factory linked it to recreational vehicle manufacturing.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://thumbwind.com/2026/03/10/brief-history-of-brown-city/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early Brown City economy hinged on lumber and agriculture, with the Brown City Elevator becoming a major grain exchange point.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://thumbwind.com/2026/03/10/brief-history-of-brown-city/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Main Street once hosted Joseph Bros.’ garment factory, which shuttered in 1927 after decades of production.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://thumbwind.com/2026/03/10/brief-history-of-brown-city/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Brown City straddles Lapeer and Sanilac counties, placing it in Michigan’s Thumb region.",
